### What is the Adapter pattern for payment integration?

The client defines its own `PaymentProcessor` interface. The third-party gateway has a different, incompatible API. The adapter implements the target interface and holds an instance of the gateway, translating each call.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public interface PaymentProcessor {
    PaymentResult pay(String accountId, BigDecimal amount);
}
```

### Why do you need an Adapter for gateway integration?

Without an adapter, your business logic is directly coupled to the vendor's API. If the vendor changes their API or you switch providers, every call site needs modification. The adapter isolates the vendor-specific details.

```java
// Without adapter: coupled to vendor (bad)
RazorpayGateway gateway = new RazorpayGateway();
RazorpayResponse resp = gateway.charge(
    new RazorpayRequest("SAV2001", 500000)); // amount in paise
// Vendor-specific types leak into your business code

// With adapter: clean interface (good)
PaymentProcessor processor = new RazorpayAdapter(new RazorpayGateway());
PaymentResult result = processor.pay("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("5000.00"));
// Business code sees only its own types
```

### How do you implement the third-party gateway?

The third-party gateway is the **adaptee**. It has its own method names, parameter types, and response format that do not match your system.

```java
package com.bank.vendor;

public class RazorpayGateway {
    public RazorpayResponse charge(RazorpayRequest request) {
        System.out.println("Razorpay: charging "
            + request.getAmountInPaise() + " paise");
        return new RazorpayResponse("rzp_txn_001", "SUCCESS");
    }
}

public class RazorpayRequest {
    private final String merchantRef;
    private final long amountInPaise; // paise, not rupees

    public RazorpayRequest(String merchantRef, long amountInPaise) {
        this.merchantRef = merchantRef;
        this.amountInPaise = amountInPaise;
    }
    public String getMerchantRef() { return merchantRef; }
    public long getAmountInPaise() { return amountInPaise; }
}

public class RazorpayResponse {
    private final String txnId;
    private final String status;
    public RazorpayResponse(String txnId, String status) {
        this.txnId = txnId; this.status = status;
    }
    public String getTxnId() { return txnId; }
    public String getStatus() { return status; }
}
```

### How do you implement the Adapter?

The adapter implements the target `PaymentProcessor` interface and translates calls to the vendor's API. It converts rupees to paise, maps arguments, and adapts the response.

```java
package com.bank.adapter;

import com.bank.service.PaymentProcessor;
import com.bank.service.PaymentResult;
import com.bank.vendor.RazorpayGateway;
import com.bank.vendor.RazorpayRequest;
import com.bank.vendor.RazorpayResponse;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class RazorpayAdapter implements PaymentProcessor {
    private final RazorpayGateway gateway;

    public RazorpayAdapter(RazorpayGateway gateway) {
        this.gateway = gateway;
    }

    @Override
    public PaymentResult pay(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        // Convert rupees to paise
        long paise = amount.multiply(BigDecimal.valueOf(100)).longValue();
        RazorpayRequest request = new RazorpayRequest(accountId, paise);
        RazorpayResponse response = gateway.charge(request);
        return new PaymentResult(
            response.getTxnId(),
            "SUCCESS".equals(response.getStatus())
        );
    }
}
```

### How do you swap to a different provider?

Writing a new adapter for a different provider requires zero changes to the business logic. This satisfies Open/Closed.

```java
package com.bank.adapter;

import com.bank.service.PaymentProcessor;
import com.bank.service.PaymentResult;
import com.bank.vendor.StripeGateway;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class StripeAdapter implements PaymentProcessor {
    private final StripeGateway gateway;

    public StripeAdapter(StripeGateway gateway) {
        this.gateway = gateway;
    }

    @Override
    public PaymentResult pay(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        // Stripe uses cents, different method name
        long cents = amount.multiply(BigDecimal.valueOf(100)).longValue();
        var response = gateway.createCharge(accountId, cents);
        return new PaymentResult(response.id(), response.paid());
    }
}
```

```java
// Switch provider by changing one line of configuration
PaymentProcessor processor = new StripeAdapter(new StripeGateway());
processor.pay("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("5000.00"));
// Business code is identical regardless of provider
```

### What should the Adapter normalise?

The adapter is the right place to tame the messy realities of third-party APIs:

- **Currency units**: Razorpay uses paise, Stripe uses cents, your system uses `BigDecimal` rupees.
- **Error codes**: each vendor has its own error format; the adapter maps them to your standard `PaymentResult`.
- **Idempotency keys**: the adapter can generate or forward idempotency tokens.
- **Retry semantics**: the adapter can wrap calls with retry logic specific to the vendor's rate limits.

```java
@Override
public PaymentResult pay(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
    try {
        long paise = amount.multiply(BigDecimal.valueOf(100)).longValue();
        RazorpayResponse resp = gateway.charge(
            new RazorpayRequest(accountId, paise));
        return new PaymentResult(resp.getTxnId(), true);
    } catch (RazorpayException e) {
        // Normalise vendor-specific error to standard result
        return new PaymentResult(null, false);
    }
}
```

### How do you wire Adapters with Spring?

Use a configuration class that creates the right adapter based on a property. The rest of the application depends on `PaymentProcessor` and never knows which vendor is behind it.

```java
package com.bank.config;

import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Value;

@Configuration
public class PaymentConfig {

    @Value("${payment.provider}")
    private String provider;

    @Bean
    public PaymentProcessor paymentProcessor() {
        return switch (provider) {
            case "razorpay" -> new RazorpayAdapter(new RazorpayGateway());
            case "stripe"   -> new StripeAdapter(new StripeGateway());
            default -> throw new IllegalArgumentException(
                "Unknown provider: " + provider);
        };
    }
}
```

### What are common mistakes?

The first mistake is letting vendor types leak into the business layer. The whole point of the adapter is isolation.

```java
// Wrong: vendor type in service signature
public void process(RazorpayResponse response) { } // coupled

// Correct: only your own types
public void process(PaymentResult result) { } // isolated
```

The second mistake is putting business logic inside the adapter. The adapter should only translate; business rules belong in the service.

### What should a senior engineer know?

The adapter is the perfect place to tame vendor quirks so that everything behind it sees one consistent, well-behaved interface. At National Bank of India, wrapping a payment gateway like Razorpay behind an adapter is exactly what lets the team onboard or swap a provider by writing one class, while the payment flow that has already been tested and audited stays completely untouched.
